[TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswb1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR OSPF on the distribution switches’ management SVI (br0.99). On dswa1 and dswb1 the br0.99 interface has both the subnet address and a /32 “virtual gateway” address configured, and OSPF is active on that SVI.

Mechanism: FRR is treating the /32 as the primary address and brings up a second, unnumbered OSPF interface context on br0.99. It then emits Hellos with a /32 mask on a broadcast LAN where peers (dswa2/dswb2) use a /28. This produces continual “NetworkMask mismatch” processing and duplicate OSPF interface contexts, which drives periodic OSPF control‑plane churn (every 10s Hello), briefly stalling routing and making campus services “freeze.”

Localization and evidence:
- dswa1 br0.99
[FINAL] stop=no-more-tool-calls tool_calls=36
[SUBMITTED] parsed_ok=True is_anomaly=True faulty_devices=['dswa1', 'dswb1']
[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswb1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR OSPF on the distribution switches’ management SVI (br0.99). On dswa1 and dswb1 the br0.99 interface has both the subnet address and a /32 “virtual gateway” address configured, and OSPF is active on that SVI.

Mechanism: FRR is treating the /32 as the primary address and brings up a second, unnumbered OSPF interface context on br0.99. It then emits Hellos with a /32 mask on a broadcast LAN where peers (dswa2/dswb2) use a /28. This produces continual “NetworkMask mismatch” processing and duplicate OSPF interface contexts, which drives periodic OSPF control‑plane churn (every 10s Hello), briefly stalling routing and making campus services “freeze.”

Localization and evidence:
- dswa1 br0.99: “This interface is UNNUMBERED… DR Interface Address 10.0.0.1/32” and also shows “Internet Address 10.0.0.2/28” with a neighbor (show ip ospf interface br0.99). ip addr confirms 10.0.0.1/32 present on br0.99.
- dswa2 FRR log repeatedly: “NetworkMask mismatch on br0.99… configured prefix length is 28, but hello packet indicates 32” from neighbor 10.0.0.79 (dswa1).
- dswb1 exhibits the same pattern with 10.0.0.17/32; dswb2 logs the same mismatch from neighbor 10.0.0.81 (dswb1).

Root cause: Secondary /32 VIPs were left on OSPF‑enabled br0.99 on dswa1 and dswb1, causing OSPF hellos with a /32 mask and duplicate OSPF interface contexts.

Corrective fix: Either remove the /32 VIPs from br0.99 (keep only the /28) or make br0.99 passive for OSPF on all DSWs and run the DSW‑DSW adjacency over the dedicated peer links (eth3/eth4) with proper /30s. Afterward, verify br0.99 shows a single, numbered OSPF interface and that the “NetworkMask mismatch” logs stop. === END ===